WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.002
[intense music]

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:06.739
 All right, bladesmiths,
your blades have been tested.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.773
They've been evaluated.

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:09.208
The judges have deliberated.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.243
And here are the standings.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.647
In fourth place, we've got
Gary earning one point.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:17.917
In third place, Gene,
you earned three points.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:24.023
And our first place
competitor is Chad.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:25.592 line:7%
You got 10 points, buddy.

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.660 line:7%
Matt, that leaves you with six.

00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:29.796 line:7%
Everybody's done fantastic work.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:33.133
And now it's time
for our second event.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.401
All right, gentlemen, over
here on our pantry shelves,

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:39.805
we've got stacks of 15
and 20 and 1095 steel.

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:41.274
What does that mean gentlemen?

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:41.974
 Damascus.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.009
 That's right.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:45.044
This is a Damascus challenge.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.812
And it's going to
be made even more

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.381
tough because in
our second event,

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:49.815
you're not making
just one blade,

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.985
you're making two
throwing knives.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:54.954
Gentlemen, do your best.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:58.957
Your three hours starts now.

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:00.527
 I've never made
it throwing knife,

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:03.596
so today is going to be my first
and second throwing knives.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.399
I'm taping up my ruler so I
can't screw up my parameters.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:08.501
All I got to do is tape
between these two blue lines

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:09.269
and I'm golden.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.807
 If you start with
good steels, it

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.776
makes it a better possibility
of getting good welds.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.244
[sizzling]

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:20.046
The only kind of throwing
weapon I've ever made before

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.915
was a tomahawk, and that's
not at all the same.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.084
 I designed kind
of a throwing knife.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.920
Got a good tip on it.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.420
But it's also going
to have a lot of meat

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.190
so that you can really feel
it when you're throwing it.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.927
 I really want to focus
on keeping it simple,

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:37.563
just really trying to
meet the parameters.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.166
It's a tall order, but I
feel confident in my skills.

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:46.005
So I'm satisfied with
the taking of my weld.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.274
So now it's time to
dance with Big Blue.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.811
Me and Big Blue, we
got a relationship now.

00:01:51.877 --> 00:01:53.546
You know, we're pretty tight.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.447
I actually narrow it
down in the center.

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.550
And I'm going to
draw up the handles.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.252
At this point,
everything's going great.

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.220
Couldn't be happier.

00:02:01.286 --> 00:02:03.389
 So it looks like my
billet is up to heat.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:06.125
I'm going to set my
welds with a hammer.

00:02:06.191 --> 00:02:08.261
It's a lot more
cautious approach to me

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:09.628
to set it by hand.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.631
But you know, I can't be
too careful at this point.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:16.136
I can really be particular on
exactly where I hit the billet

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.005
and not being too violent
by going over to Big Blue

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:20.406
and just smashing
the heck out of it.

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:25.611
 I really need to get
the billet nice and hot

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:28.114
so that these pieces will
all forge well together.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.184
So I don't to rush this
part of the process.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:33.352
If they don't set, I'm not
going to be able to get

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.521
two knives out of the steel.

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:39.859
 I get my steel hot and
I'm over to the press--

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:42.262
Oh no, these welds didn't hold.

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.397
It's kind of like if you're
making that big sandwich,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.232
take a big bite, and everything
comes out the other damn end,

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:48.667
and it's all over the floor.

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:51.537
So now I'm looking at my
billet, it's not forge welded.

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:55.207
And I'm going to have to
figure this out right now.

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.677
I can't come in fourth
because that's going to put

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.645
me out of the competition.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:02.181
This time, I get it really hot
and allow the width of that

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:05.084
die to sandwich it down.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.787
I do feel like it's
starting to weld to itself,

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.255
which is the ultimate goal.

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:10.522
But it's going to have to
go right back in the heat.

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:12.291
I'm going to have to
repeat this process

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:15.962
a few times before I can
really start drawing this out.

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.496
 My game plan is
try to get these two

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:20.066
knives as similar as possible.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.701
Now I have two
identical throwing

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:23.402
knives at 12 inches long.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:24.938
I realized that
they're real heavy.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.472
I want them heavy.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.106
You have to throw them real
hard, but man, do they stick.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.144
And they make a
really loud thunk.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.912
 So I finally get
my steel drawn out.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.480
Cut them in half.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.449
I want to quench
my blades early,

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:39.919
so that's going to
guarantee me a hard edge.

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:42.121
I wouldn't call them knives yet.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.990
They're more like bricks.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.826
 I feel good, but
I know that it's

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.028
still really anyone's game.

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.395
I make the decision
to just quench

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.231
the tip, thinking
that's the only

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:53.766
critical area of the blade.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.736
I have one of my knives cooling
down, so what I need to do

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:58.905
is set up another vice
at another station.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.140
So I go to the scrap pit,
and I'm going to go find two

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:03.709
matching pieces of angle iron.

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:04.643
Ow!

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:05.578
[bleep]

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:08.214
I snagged the inside
of my left arm.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.382
I'm that guy.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.383
I'm bleeding on
"Forged in Fire."

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:17.823
 I'm just going to do a
little bit of an edge quench.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.092
There's no need for
the tang or the side

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.326
of the knife to be hard.

00:04:21.426 --> 00:04:22.895
I mean, we're throwing
these things around,

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:25.130
so you don't want them to break.

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:26.732
As I'm starting to
finish up and realizing

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:29.535
that they're pretty similar,
it's what I was shooting for.

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:33.806
But things don't always work out
when you only have three hours.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:37.543
 My plan is to heat treat
one knife and then the other.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.546
I'm just going to harden maybe
the last quarter of the knife

00:04:40.612 --> 00:04:41.881
because that's the
part that's going

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.683
to be digging into the wood.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.583
 I feel pretty good.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:46.019
You know, we're
down to the wire,

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.386
so I'm going to have
to keep hustling.

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:49.354
But I'm going to work
till the last second.

00:04:49.454 --> 00:04:52.191
 So I've got the edges
kind of profiled in.

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.759
It's time to start
working on the handle.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:58.564
But the problem is the buffing
bell took my edge with it.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.266
 Oh boy.

00:05:00.332 --> 00:05:01.934
 I don't have time to fix it.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:08.274
So here I am again stuck with
some bad on-the-spot decisions.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:13.179
 5, 4, 3, 2, 1!

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:14.880
Bladesmiths, stop
what you're doing.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.248
Shut down your machines.

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:17.616
Drop your tools.

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:21.253
[applause]

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.689
 I'm frustrated,
I'm disappointed.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:26.626
But I'm also relieved because
I was concerned I wasn't going

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:27.927
to be able to turn anything in.

00:05:30.796 --> 00:05:33.132
 All right, gentlemen,
welcome to the knife throwing

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:35.468
triple threat.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:36.635
All right, Gene,
you're up first year.

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:37.637
Are you ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:40.573
[intense music]

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.480
[thunking]

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.418
[clanking]

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:02.828
 All right, so Gene, right
off, a lot of weight to these.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:06.031
So a fairly classic shape
overall for a thrower.

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:09.802
What I do appreciate is that
the handles are even in size.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.904
The handles feel
the same regardless

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.406
of the knife, which is really
what I was looking for.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.208
When we moved to
the harder target,

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.910
this knife did take
a bit of damage,

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:20.145
and you did lose just
a tip right there.

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:21.747
Just packed out a little bit.

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:24.117
But these didn't start out with
a lot of point on them, anyway.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:26.085
But they threw well.

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:27.419
Good job.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.553
- Thank you, sir.
- All right, Chad, you're up.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:29.455
You ready for this?
- Yes, sir.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:30.590
 OK, let's do this.

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:35.495
[intense music]

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:39.399
[thunking]

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.769
[clanking]

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:51.677
All right, so Chad, I
think you nailed it.

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.746
These are a great
weight, a great size.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:56.415
Feel super comfortable
in my hand.

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:57.850
They match really nicely.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:00.485
This is something that's
super important in throwing.

00:07:00.585 --> 00:07:04.089
Your tips, they hit
into the clay pigeons,

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:05.157
didn't lose any tip.

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:06.992
Just a great job all around.

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:07.793
Nicely done.

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:08.461
 Thank you.

00:07:08.527 --> 00:07:09.428
 Gary, you're up.

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:10.229
You ready for this?

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:10.963
 Let's do it.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.932
 OK.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:15.435
[thunking]

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:26.979
[shattering]

00:07:31.317 --> 00:07:34.419
So right off, when I looked
at these, I was like, nah,

00:07:34.519 --> 00:07:35.421
I don't like them.

00:07:35.487 --> 00:07:37.856
[laughs] I'm proven wrong again.

00:07:37.923 --> 00:07:41.260
[laughs] These actually
throw really good.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.063
I did not think
this skinny dropped

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:45.865
handle would throw well.

00:07:45.931 --> 00:07:47.600
It actually feels
better like that.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.469
I was throwing them with the
curve going up into my hand

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:51.470
as opposed to down.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.771
They throw really nicely.

00:07:52.871 --> 00:07:54.540
They've got a really
sharp point on them.

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:57.276
When it penetrates, it just
buries itself really nicely.

00:07:57.376 --> 00:08:00.913
The back of the handle, there's
a couple of spots on here

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.448
that I can feel.

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:04.383
They didn't really drag
when it left my hand.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.418
But they threw really
well, so good job.

00:08:06.485 --> 00:08:07.553
 Thank you.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.955
 All right, Matt,
you're up next.

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:10.989
Are you ready to go?

00:08:11.056 --> 00:08:11.724
 Sure.

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:16.596
[thunking]

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:20.933
Nice.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.268
[clanking]

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:26.239
 Nice one, Matt.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:27.773
 Thanks, brother.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.675
 So Matt, these
things are heavy.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:33.545
These have what I like
to call the brick factor.

00:08:33.612 --> 00:08:35.214
It's like throwing a brick.

00:08:35.281 --> 00:08:37.549
It doesn't matter if you
hit it with a point or not,

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:38.917
whoever you hit is going down.

00:08:38.984 --> 00:08:40.819
The blades themselves
don't quite match.

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:42.888
You've got one lean blade
and one wider blade.

00:08:42.988 --> 00:08:46.258
But what I do like is the
handles match beautifully

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:47.859
and they really
fit my hand well.

00:08:47.959 --> 00:08:50.796
They do have a very
cute point in profile.

00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:52.998
I would have loved to have
seen a little bit leaner,

00:08:53.098 --> 00:08:56.701
but I mean, it's hard to
argue with how easy it

00:08:56.801 --> 00:08:58.237
was to get accurate with these.

00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:02.374
As far as being tough, your
points are still there.

00:09:02.474 --> 00:09:03.175
Well done.

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:04.476
 Thank you, sir.

00:09:04.543 --> 00:09:07.547
[intense music]

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:11.617
 All right, bladesmiths, the
second event in the Summer

00:09:11.683 --> 00:09:13.585
Forging Games is
complete, and the judges

00:09:13.652 --> 00:09:14.987
need to have a deliberation.

00:09:15.087 --> 00:09:18.424
I remind you that after
this deliberation,

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:19.959
the person with
the lowest points

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:22.261
will be eliminated
from the games.

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:24.062
Bladesmiths, we need
the forge floor.

00:09:24.129 --> 00:09:26.231
Thank you.

00:09:26.298 --> 00:09:29.435
All right, bladesmiths, the
second event in our summer

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:31.003
forging games is complete.

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:33.338
The judges' deliberation
is complete.

00:09:33.438 --> 00:09:35.907
And they have ranked your work.

00:09:36.007 --> 00:09:39.312 line:0%
In first place, Chad,
congratulations.

00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:40.679 line:0%
That's 10 more
points to you, buddy,

00:09:40.745 --> 00:09:43.815 line:7%
giving you a point total of 20.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:48.086 line:0%
In second place, Gary,
our come-from-behind guy.

00:09:48.186 --> 00:09:51.357 line:0%
you came out of that first
round with just one point

00:09:51.457 --> 00:09:53.159 line:0%
and now you've
made up another six

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:55.327 line:7%
points, giving you seven total.

00:09:55.394 --> 00:10:00.131
Our third place
finisher is Matt.

00:10:00.232 --> 00:10:01.467
Congratulations.

00:10:01.534 --> 00:10:03.435 line:0%
That's three points to
you adding to your six

00:10:03.502 --> 00:10:04.769 line:7%
from the first event.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.473 line:0%
That gives you
nine points total.

00:10:07.573 --> 00:10:10.076 line:0%
And our fourth place
finisher is Gene.

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:13.846 line:0%
You came in third
on the first event

00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:16.415 line:0%
and here coming in fourth
in the second event

00:10:16.482 --> 00:10:18.917 line:0%
gives you one point
for a total of four.

00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:21.654
Gene, you came out with
the least number of points

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.088
after two events.

00:10:23.188 --> 00:10:26.258
Unfortunately, at this
time, you've been eliminated

00:10:26.358 --> 00:10:29.394
from the Summer Forging Games.

00:10:29.461 --> 00:10:30.929
 My knives performed poorly.

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:32.631
The other bladesmiths
did a better job,

00:10:32.731 --> 00:10:33.665
and I'm cool with that.

00:10:33.765 --> 00:10:34.934
 Hey, it was a good fight, man.

00:10:35.001 --> 00:10:35.801
 Thank you, sir

00:10:35.901 --> 00:10:37.369
 I've learned a
lot and I'm just

00:10:37.436 --> 00:10:40.272
excited to go back home, put
that new knowledge to work,

00:10:40.338 --> 00:10:42.741
become a better bladesmith, and
hopefully come back one day,

00:10:42.807 --> 00:10:45.277
and show these judges
what I'm capable of.

